teral — located through helmprov's spans: `r.sites[i].at` = { \"templates/x.yaml:74\" }), `absent`, or `mixed` (which documents differ is named); `r.knobs` = chart placeholders where every manifest agrees and nothing varies below. ⚠ \"varies\" is relative to the corpus (one deployment per service makes a service name a constant); keyed alignment is only as good as the merge key (loadgenerator's `main` container is not `server`). Measured on microservices-demo (helm-chart vs kubernetes-manifests): 35 paired documents, 25 varying spots — 8 parameter, 4 block, 11 HARDCODED (container port, PORT env, gRPC probe ports, probe timings, Service type and ports: 81 template lines), 2 mixed (the chart's redis-cart Deployment and frontend-external Service lack the `app` label the manifests carry), 19 knobs never turned (peer service names in the ADDR env values). CART-0873 |\n| SPECIALIZE a Lua function to some of its arguments — what does it become when they are known (a partial evaluator; an interpreter specialized to its program = that program compiled)? | `local MX = require 'cartograph.mix'; local text = MX.mix(require('cartograph.algebraread').read(<src>, 'lua'), <fname>, { 'S' \\| 'D' per parameter }, { <static values> })` → the RESIDUAL program as Lua text (`return <entry>`; re-read it through algebraread and `load` it): offline mix — `MX.lower` (term → IR, scopes resolved), `MX.bta` (per function and division; a variable assigned under DYNAMIC control is dynamic), `MX.specialize` (static parts computed by mix's own interpreter `MX.run`, static loops unrolled, a call with dynamic arguments a memoized PROGRAM POINT or UNFOLDED when its residual is one `return e`), `MX.print`. Rung 1 is FIRST-ORDER on the subset S: closures, while, varargs, method calls, static tables reaching dynamic code and runaway specialization (a static value that never repeats) are REFUSED by name; mix itself stays inside S (a test fences it: S4–S5 self-apply it). Gate: power(x, 5) → `x*(x*(x*(x*(x*1))))`; a stack-machine interpreter specialized to `{add 3, mulx, add 1}` → `(3*x)+1` (96 → 6 interpreter steps); its `jnz` loop → one residual recursive function; equivalence, no static name surviving, and the step drop all asserted; 5/5 mutations (a forced static, dropped congruence, all-dynamic BTA, no unfold, no memo) caught. CART-1278 |\n","before":"| Where does a Helm chart HARDCODE what varies across the plain manifests it should produce — the lines to extend the chart at — and which of its knobs does the corpus never turn? | `:CartographHelmBase {manifests} [chart]` (quickfix: one item per template line holding a literal where the manifests vary). In Lua `local B = require 'cartograph.helmbase'; local r = B.diff(<chart>, <manifests dir>); print(table.concat(B.lines(r), '\\n'))` — the AUTHORED base is the chart rendered with no values (helmprov symbolic), the INFERRED base the manifests' keyed generalization (`kv_generalize`, per kind); documents pair by FILE NAME (`templates/<x>.yaml` with `<manifests>/<x>.yaml`) and by ORDINAL within a kind (an authored name is a placeholder). Every spot that VARIES across the manifests is, in the chart, `param` (a placeholder there, also inside a string), `block` (a placeholder above it: `toYaml .Values.x.resources`), `hardcoded` (a literal — located through helmprov's spans: `r.sites[i].at` = { \"templates/x.yaml:74\" }), `absent`, or `mixed` (which documents differ is named); `r.knobs` = chart placeholders where every manifest agrees and nothing varies below. ⚠ \"varies\" is relative to the corpus (one deployment per service makes a service name a constant); keyed alignment is only as good as the merge key (loadgenerator's `main` container is not `server`). Measured on microservices-demo (helm-chart vs kubernetes-manifests): 35 paired documents, 25 varying spots — 8 parameter, 4 block, 11 HARDCODED (container port, PORT env, gRPC probe ports, probe timings, Service type and ports: 81 template lines), 2 mixed (the chart'